<options source="ScorchDroid">
  <!-- M2 vertical slice: a fast local practice-vs-bots game. This is a
       ScorchDroid-owned config, not part of upstream's data/ - see the
       porting plan (AssetDataExtractor / initEngine). -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfMinPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M5 Phase 2: OptionsGame.cpp's own default NoMaxPlayers is 2, which
       addHumanTank() + the single starting bot already fill on their own -
       ServerConnectAuthHandler::processMessageInternal() rejects
       ("Server full") any real network client trying to join once
       getNoMaxPlayers() - getNoOfTanks() <= 0, found the hard way testing
       a real two-emulator join. Raised well past 2 so a host still has
       room for real LAN players, not just its own local bot-practice tank. -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfPlayers</name>
    <value>8</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>RemoveBotsAtPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M4: engine_jni.cpp's addHumanTank() adds one real (destinationId != 0)
       "my tank" player itself, counted by
       ServerStateEnoughPlayers::countBots() exactly like a real network
       client would be - so only one more bot slot is needed to reach the
       2-player total above. PlayerType1 left as "Human" (the OptionsGame.cpp
       default) so ballanceBots() doesn't also try to add a bot into that
       slot. -->
  <option>
    <name>PlayerType2</name>
    <value>Moron</value>
  </option>
  <!-- TurnSimultaneous (0): every tank can move/fire within the same round
       window, rather than strict turn order (the default,
       TurnSequentialLoserFirst) - simplifies touch-fire, which just directly
       submits a move for whichever tank is tapped, with no turn-order
       bookkeeping of our own. -->
  <option>
    <name>TurnType</name>
    <value>0</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>ShotTime</name>
    <value>35</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>BuyingTime</name>
    <value>30</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>StartTime</name>
    <value>5</value>
  </option>

  <!-- ############################################################
       TESTING ONLY - REMOVE BEFORE RELEASE
       ############################################################
       Starting money raised from the engine default of 10000 to 100000 so
       the whole shop is reachable in round one while the weapon effects
       (napalm, laser, lightning) are still being checked on device - most
       of them cost far more than a real starting balance affords.

       This trivialises the economy, which is a real part of the game: with
       this in place there is no reason to save, and the buying phase stops
       being a decision. Delete this whole block before any release build.
       Tracked in the porting plan under "temporary testing settings".
       ############################################################ -->
  <option>
    <name>MoneyStarting</name>
    <value>100000</value>
  </option>

  <!-- Also testing-only, same block, same removal. Upstream's default is 2:
       "the first round players are allowed to buy on", so round one has no
       buying phase at all and you fight with the default loadout. That is
       real upstream behaviour, not a bug - but it means a fresh game can
       never reach the shop, which makes testing anything bought tedious.

       Worth revisiting on its own merits before release: a first round with
       no shop reads as broken on a phone, where there is no manual telling
       you otherwise. Changing it is a balance decision though, not a port
       fix, so it stays inside the temporary block for now. -->
  <option>
    <name>MoneyBuyOnRound</name>
    <value>1</value>
  </option>
</options>
